Blueprint Before the Build
Before a single line of code gets deployed, there’s a mental model you need to hold — a system where every layer exists for a reason and feeds into the next. At the foundation sits a React + Node.js + PostgreSQL application, split into 7 microservices.
These aren’t monolith pieces carelessly sliced apart, each service owns its domain, communicates over well-defined boundaries, and can fail independently without dragging the rest down. That separation is intentional — it’s the foundation everything else is built on.
From Code to Cloud, Without Touching a Server
The moment code is pushed to GitHub, the system wakes up. GitHub Actions picks it up, validates it, builds Docker images and pushes them to the registry — all without a single manual step. But what makes this architecture interesting isn’t just the automation, it’s what decides what gets deployed. That’s ArgoCD.
With GitOps, the cluster doesn’t wait for instructions — it continuously watches the Git repository, and if the desired state in Git doesn’t match what’s running in the cluster, it self-corrects. Instead of a pipeline pushing changes into the cluster, the cluster itself pulls the desired state from Git
The infrastructure is managed by Terraform, the cluster runs on AWS EKS handles environment-specific config layering. Infrastructure stops being something you touch — it becomes something you declare.
Observability as a First-Class Citizen
A system you can’t see is a system you can’t trust. Three questions every engineer in production should always be able to answer: Is the system up? How fast is it responding? And how will you know when something breaks?
Prometheus scrapes metrics from every running service, Grafana turns those numbers into dashboards you can actually read and AWS Fluent Bit forwards logs straight into CloudWatch — each one answering a piece of that question.
This isn’t bolted on at the end, it’s wired into the architecture from day one. Because in production, the question isn’t if something breaks — it’s how fast you know.
The Intelligence Layer: Where AIOps Changes the Game
This is where the architecture steps beyond standard DevOps. Sitting above the observability stack is Kira — an AWS Bedrock Agent trained to do what humans are slow at: correlate signals across logs, metrics and traces at scale, and surface what actually matters.
Instead of an engineer wading through Grafana alerts at midnight, Kira can analyze anomalies, identify likely root causes and suggest remediation steps. The shift is subtle but profound — the system doesn’t just report what’s wrong anymore, it starts reasoning about it. That’s the AIOps layer, and it’s what turns a well-built pipeline into a genuinely intelligent platform.
WorkFlow
Every system that looks elegant from the outside is a series of deliberate decisions made on the inside. This one has six workflows — each one a layer that the previous one made possible.

