Before any pipeline, any cluster, any CI/CD — there’s an architectural decision that either sets you up or haunts you: how do you structure the application?


The answer here was microservices — not because it’s trendy, but because it forces discipline. The Boutique app is an e-commerce web store, and if you’ve seen the UI, it looks deceptively simple: browse products, add to cart, place an order. But underneath that clean interface, 5 backend services are running independently — Auth, Product, Orders, Order Management and User — each living on its own port (3002–3006), owning its own logic, and talking to nothing it doesn’t need to.

A React frontend on port 3000 never speaks to a service directly. Every request flows through an API Gateway on port 3001, which acts as the single front door — routing, controlling, and shielding the services behind it. Nginx sits in front as the reverse proxy, handling what the public internet sees before anything reaches application code.

What makes this more than just a “split-up app” is the database strategy. A single PostgreSQL instance runs at port 5432, but it’s not one shared blob of data — it’s logically separated into distinct databases per domain: auth_db, products_db, and so on. Each service only touches its own slice. This matters enormously later when you're isolating failures, debugging in production, or deciding which service to scale. The architecture here wasn't accidental — it was the prerequisite for everything that followed.

Workflow 1 — Local Dev with Docker Compose
