https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1100/format:webp/1*r4dg8mZK3T-BhVbBxsOIlA.png


There’s a moment every developer knows — the app runs perfectly on their machine and immediately breaks on someone else’s. Docker Compose is the answer to that moment.

With a single docker-compose up --build, the entire system — frontend, API Gateway, all five services, PostgreSQL, Prometheus and Grafana — spins up on a shared Docker internal network. Every container can reach every other container by name, not by IP. No manual port configuration, no "did you install the right version of Node?" — the environment is the same for everyone, every time.

PostgreSQL pulls a public image and initializes four logical databases on first boot. Prometheus and Grafana aren't an afterthought bolted on for production — they're running at ports 9090 and 3007 from day one locally, which means observability is something you develop with, not something you scramble to add later.

What’s worth pausing on here is what Docker Compose actually taught about the system. Running everything together locally exposed communication patterns between services, revealed which services were stateful, and made it clear how Nginx needed to be configured before a single Kubernetes manifest was written. Local environment wasn’t just a convenience — it was a rehearsal.

Workflow 2 — Cloud Infrastructure with Terraform

